title: Colonic Polyps and Polyposis Syndromes
---

# Colonic Polyps and Polyposis Syndromes

- **Polyps** - raised lesions along the colonic epithelium, which may be neoplastic or non-neoplastic
- **Classification of colonic polyps:**
  - **Non-neoplastic polyps:**
    - <u>Harmatomatous polyps</u> - polyps with mucosal cells but growing in a disorganised mass
    - <u>Hyperplastic polyps</u> - characteristically serrated polyps with proliferative characteristics and no cellular dysplasia
    - <u>Inflammatory polyps</u> - intraluminal protection of mucosal consisting of stromal, epithelial components rich in inflammatory cells
  - **Neoplastic polyps** = adenomatous polyps, sessile serrated polyps and traditional serrated adenomas
- **Clinical features:**
  - <u>Asymptomatic</u> - most are asymptomatic with incidental finding on routine screening or cross-sectional imaging of other intent
  - <u>PR bleeding or anaemia</u> - occurs in large polyps
  - <u>Tenesmus</u> - if polyp in rectum
  - <u>Intestinal obstruction</u> - for large polyps
  - <u>Diarrhoea and hypokalaemia</u> - due to secretion of mucous by villous adenomas
- **Endoscopic classification of polyps** - Paris Classification:
  - <u>Sessile</u> - polypoid lesions where the base and top of the lesion have the same diameter (granular mucosa is a feature of benign tumour)
  - <u>Pedunculated</u> - polypoid lesion with a narrow base resulting in a mucosal stalk interposed between the polyp and the wall
  - <u>Flat</u> - polypoidal lesions with height \< 50% of diameter of lesion
  - <u>Depressed</u> - lesions where entire thickness of mucosa in the lesion is less than that of the adjacent mucosa

  
  ![](../img/paris_classification_diagram.png)
- **Endoscopic features suggestive of invasive cancer:**
  - <u>Friability</u>
  - <u>Induration</u>
  - <u>Ulceration</u>
- **Histological classification of adenomatous polyps:**
  - **By architecture:**
    - <u>Tubular adenoma</u> (80%) - adenoma with \>= 75% tubular component with long branching adenomatous epithelium organised in tubules
    - <u>Villous adenoma</u> (5-15%) - adenoma with \>= 75% villous component with glands that are long and extend straight down from the surface to the center of the polyp
    - <u>Tubulovillous adenoma</u> (5-15%) - adenoma with 25-75% villous features
  - **By differentiation:**
    - <u>Low-grade dysplasia</u> - low risk of malignant transformation
    - <u>High-grade dysplasia</u> (intraepithelial carcinoma) - intermmediate step in progression from low-grade dysplasia and invasive cancer
- **Features of advanced adenomas** - high risk of metachronous CRC or polyp:
  - <u>Large polyps</u> - \> 10mm or \> 20mm
  - <u>Villous architecture</u> - villous components have higher risk of malignant transformation
  - <u>Poor differentiation</u> - high-grade dysplasia
  - <u>Multiple polyps</u>
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Endoscopic removal</u> - indicated for all polyps
    - <u>Colonoscopy</u> - screen entire bowel if polyps discovered by sigmoidoscopy (40-50% patients have proximal polyps)
    - <u>Surveillance</u> - closer surveillance for selected patients after dependent on whether there is advanced adenomas
    - <u>Segmental colectomy</u> - presence of malignancy w/ risk factors of residual cancer and/or nodal metastasis
  - **Endoscopic removal:**
    - **Polypectomy** - complete removal of small adenomas using biopsy forceps
    - **Endoscopic resection techniques** (EMR, ESD) - reserved for large, sessile polyps
  - **Surveillance** - risk assessment for subsequent metachronous tumour or polyp, based on size, number, histology and differentiation:
    - **Advanced adenomas:**
      - <u>First surveillance</u> - at 3y (or 6mo if large polyps requiring piecemeal resection)
      - <u>Subsequent resection</u> - q3-5y based on 1st surveillance findings (shorter intervals for higher risk patients, e.g. advanced adenomas, or increased number of non-advanced adenomas)
    - **Non-advanced adenomas:**
      - <u>First surveillance</u> - q7-10y
      - <u>Subsequent surveillance</u> - interval depends on number of adenomas developed, and any hereditary risk of CRC
  - **Segmental colectomy** - for early CRC with high risk of residual disease or nodal metastasis:
    - <u>Indications</u> - malignant polyps (T1, i.e. invasion through muscularis mucosa) with certain features:
      - **Resection margins** - cancer at resection margins
      - **Differentiation** - <u>poorly differentiated histology</u>
      - **Evidence of metastasis** - <u>lymphovascular invasion, perineural invasion</u>, or tumour budding (foci of isolated cancer cells at the invasive margin of the polyp)
      - **Submucosal invasion depth** - \> 1mm
- **Gastrointestinal polyposis syndromes** - hereditary syndromes characterised by development of many polyps along GI tract, classified into neoplastic and non-neoplastic disorders, based on histological architecture of polyps: 
![](../img/polyposis_syndromes.png)
  - Familial adenomatous polyposis - see notes on FAP in hereditary colorectal cancer syndromes
  - **Puetz-Jeghers syndrome** - Truncating mutation in serine-treonine kinase gene on chromosome 10p (and others) causing <u>multiple hamartomatous polyps in SB and LB</u> as well as <u>melanin pigmentation in the lips, mouth and digits</u>:
    - <u>Clinical features</u> - asymptomatic, SB bleeding, anaemia, or intususception
    - <u>Diagnosis</u> - requires 2 of 3 following:
      - SB polyposis
      - Mucocutaneous pigmentation
      - FHx suggesting autosomal dominance
    - <u>Malignancy risk</u> - slight increased risk of SB and colorectal carcinoma, and CA pancreas, lungs, ovary, breast, and endometrium
    - <u>Mx</u> - regular malignancy screen by upper and lower endoscopy, SB and pancreatic imaging
  - **Juvenile polyposis** - tens to hundreds of mucus-filled hamartomatous polyps found in colorectum, although non-neoplastic, a/w an increased risk of colorectal cancer
    - <u>Diagnosis</u> - 1) \>= 10 colonic juvenile polyps, 2) juvenile polyps elsewhere in GI tract, or 3) +ve FHx of polyps
    - <u>Malignancy risk</u> - up to 20% develop colorectal carcinoma \< 40y
    - <u>Mx</u> - regular colonoscopy and polypectomy q1-3y +/- colectomy considered
